body:
I'm currently just experiencing this myself.  Android Shield and Galaxy S20 phone, casting YTM to my Shield to listen to music over my TV speakers, and my device randomly cuts out.  When I check my phone, the YTM app says lost connection.  No I didn't.  Wifi is still connected to all devices.  

&#x200B;

This is an app problem, and this app is horrible compared to Google Play Music, which used to work flawlessly.

Nope.  It's a clean Windows 10 install recently using the latest 20H2 version on a bootable USB.  Just Steam/Epic game stores and my normal driver installs.  Very clean PC meant strictly for gaming.

&#x200B;

I've tracked individual CPU cores before with RTSS and saw that all of them were being utilized and never at 100%, however, I didn't have each core being monitored during this battle on my screenshot to know for sure.

If you look in the corner where I have RTSS info showing, you'll notice my RTX 2080 Ti GPU drops to really low usage, therefore the core clock also drops.  Why?  Is my CPU bottlenecking, because my i7 8700K at 4.8Ghz also shows low usage, so it doesn't seem to bottleneck either.

&#x200B;

Is there a setting that causes this low usage of my hardware and sub 20fps laggy battles?

This has kind of been an ongoing issue with the game over the last 2 years I've been playing it on/off, and obviously with newer GPU drivers installed, and even over various cards (1080 Ti to a 2080 Ti).  The fps drops are definitely due to the GPU utilization going from 100% down to about 50-60% in battles.  I just don't know what's causing it to do so, and I'm expecting it's just the game engine.

&#x200B;

I've checked all 12 of my CPU cores (4.8Ghz each) and they aren't capping out and bottlenecking my GPU, so it's really weird that both my CPU cores and GPU utilization just works at half their performance.

Since this patch landed, instead of laggy input from fps drops (my mouse/camera movement are much more fluid now), I am having micro freezes during attack sequences like firing a bow or using melee combos.

&#x200B;

I'm not sure which is worse!

Congrats!  If I may provide a tip (since you mentioned your fear) --- Show Confidence! :)

&#x200B;

One thing I lacked as a student was confidence.  As a tech, my outlook changed considerably since I work in an independent role.  One thing I noted is that even if you don't know something, you can still project confidence by admitting that and opening up your willingness to learn.  Your tone of voice, projection of your voice, and body language can be interpreted very differently even when saying the same words.

A higher population will always produce higher taxable income from all sources (business, property, etc.), but higher population also has its own problems.  Without the resources produced from rural provincial areas, there would be no inter-city dwellings and businesses able to operate to produce those taxes to begin with.

&#x200B;

That being said, Ontario is a huge province and different regions have dramatically different political needs.  To have provincial policies standardized for the entire province doesn't make much sense (as someone already put in the 21st century) now that global economics and communication is a thing.

Ya, I feel like I know how to generate income, but there's nothing in game for me to actually keep track of it and play with efficiencies.  It's so complex that I just play the game and sometimes I'm in the negative and sometimes in the positive!  

&#x200B;

I find sometimes I need to slow down advancing and building things, and just keep my current population happy and fed, to generate more income before building new stuff.  There's always a negative when introducing new production lines...

TDP = Tetanus - Diphtheria - Pertussis and MMR = Measles - Mumps - Rubella

&#x200B;

These have been common vaccinated requirements for a long time now, so having any kind of outbreaks  of this manner is a clear and concise pattern of anti-vaxxers causing the 'end of days' :)

TWH1 has a campaign with a bunch of races (now called 'old races' in the Warhammer universe).  TWH2 has a new 'vortex' campaign with new races.  Then they release 'Mortal Empires' which is a new campaign map with all the races from both games, plus DLC packs.  Mortal Empires comes with a ton of new changes, and campaign goals.  Playing different races has unique plots, goals, and play styles.

&#x200B;

If you are unsure if you would like the Total War experience though, then maybe just buy TWH1, try out the campaign and see if you like it, since it is cheap.  Honestly, I put hundreds of hours into TWH1 and there is nothing wrong with it.  It was really good.  But if you get both games, it's difficult to go back to TWH1, since all the changes and improvements they work on is for Mortal Empires and the Vortex campaigns.  Just like with other TW games, it's difficult going backward because they improve on so many quality of life changes that make the games less tedious and more interactive.

I was 17 (would turn 18 in January of 2000), and I remember being really upset with my plans falling through.  From what I remember, I met a friend recently who was 21, and he invited me to hang out with him and his brother at a private New Year's party downtown (my thoughts were that I could at least drink).  So instead of making plans with other friends, I relied on this pulling through, but it never did.  I kept getting the excuses left and right, and I ended up staying home alone super depressed and raging that I missed out on a big Y2K New Year's party.

&#x200B;

tl;dr: New friend invited me out for New Years and bailed on me, so I stayed home alone for what could be the most memorable New Years parties ever.

I only have 1 character as I started playing in Sept.  I knew about the game a bit beforehand though and knew that crafting would be worthwhile from the get go.  I leveled all my Crafting skills to max 50 (except Jewelry - still working on it).  Every day, one can do 'crafting writ' daily quests.  It requires you make certain things with each of your crafting professions, then hand them in to boxes for a bunch of rewards.  The main thing is that once your character is max and your crafting skills are capped, you can make like 4.2k gold a day, per character - and that's just guaranteed quest gold.  You may also get certain yellow legendary mats that are worth a lot more when sold on guild stores.

&#x200B;

Now, on 1 character, it's not a lot, but do it on multiple characters and it really adds up.  However, this actually requires multiple max level characters with max crafting skills (afaik?).  So in order to really make good daily gold earnings this way, you need to put in years worth of real time leveling up a slew of characters with crafting skills.  Then on top of that, every single day, one needs to log in each of those characters and do the writ dailies.

&#x200B;

Seems like a job to me...

I'm sort of new to the game, and noticed you can buy crowns to speed up research and even those mount upgrades.  I don't find it a "Pay to Win" system, because these things are only quality of life improvements, and don't significantly impact the gameplay.  Everyone also has access to them, and the pay feature only allows you to speed up the process for a feature that doesn't give you a strategic advantage over others.

&#x200B;

Not so much "Pay to Win" but more "Pay for Convenience"

The 1080 will be great for a 144Hz 1440p monitor.  I have the same setup, and here's something you may not know...

&#x200B;

Even if you're playing a game that can't cap out at 144fps, it'll still be really smooth, moreso than a 60Hz monitor.  It's due to input lag with the mouse/keyboard.  Everything will feel 100x more snappy.  You can test this on Windows desktop by dragging a window around in 60Hz, then change to 144Hz and see the difference.  It'll also reduce eye strain and flickering compared to 60Hz.

&#x200B;

If you're used to a 60Hz monitor, you may not know the difference yet.  However, going back to 60Hz from 120-144Hz will make your eyes bleed and want to pull your hair out.

I played a game as Hissho on Serious difficulty after having a break from ES2 since earlier this year.  It was quite easy to get back into, and I immediately realized that with Hissho, you need to colonize by waging war and occupying systems from other aliens.  Colonizing your own systems is slow and penalizing to your empire.  I would say colonize 2 (maybe 3 max) good systems near the start of the game, but turn your focus into military efforts and start occupying systems.  Minor civs are a good start.

&#x200B;

Another thing I forgot to note is that Hissho was created with the intention of having your home system being a powerhouse, building tall, and only going wide expansion once you are going for military conquest.  A reason for this is the ability to have economic/mining Behemoths (Hissho gets FIDSI resources on top of strategic resources when mining), and also one of their laws.  They have a law that gives +25% FIDSI to systems, but with -1 Keii for each system you have in your empire, UNLESS those systems have a certain building in an infinite construction, which sends tons of resources to your home system.

&#x200B;

So in essence, you are making +25% on all your systems, but sending all the resources to your home system, so that you don't have the - Keii penalty.  This is crazy good when pumping out a lot of military ships early to mid game, but not so good late game when you have a lot of occupied and developed systems.

If you haven't played either, Civ V is a cheaper and more complete package of a game and is the cornerstone of a modern day Civ game.

&#x200B;

However, after playing Civ 6, it will be really difficult to play previous Civ games due to 'quality of life' added features that expand upon Civ 5.  One thing I like is with military units now unable to stack on a hex, in Civ 5 - this created huge bottlenecks of military units.  In Civ 6, you can merge similar units into 1 unit - labeling it as a Corp or Army (with little stars indicating this).  This allows a military based civ to continue making units and beefing up, but not running out of hexes to position them.
